{"command":"# HOME override didn't create /tmp/fakehome cache, still used /home/agent/.cache. So cache dir is hardcoded to os.UserCacheDir? Which respects XDG_CACHE_HOME and HOME? But our test showed neither respected. Hmm, maybe it uses user.Current().HomeDir? That would still be /home/agent regardless of env? Or it caches based on ... Let's check: Go's os.UserCacheDir() on Linux returns $XDG_CACHE_HOME or $HOME/.cache. Our XDG test should have used /tmp/myxdg, but it didn't. So not os.UserCacheDir. Maybe it uses `os.UserHomeDir()` + "/.cache/goimports-reviser"? os.UserHomeDir also respects $HOME. But HOME override didn't work either. Could be that binary was built with `user.Current()` which reads /etc/passwd, not env? Or maybe cache path is hardcoded to `~/.cache` via homedir lib that doesn't respect env in this context? Let's test more: check if binary reads env at all? Try strace? Not allowed on original binary. But we can test behavior: does it use HOME? We saw no. Could be it uses `os.Getenv("HOME")` but we passed HOME=/tmp/fakehome correctly? We did `HOME=/tmp/fakehome /workspace/executable ...` which should set env. But no cache created there. Could be that cache dir creation failed silently? /tmp/fakehome exists, but no .cache subdir created. Maybe it requires .cache to exist? No, earlier it created ~/.cache/goimports-reviser automatically.
